-- title:
--   A parameter-initializing color driver decides distinct-index 3SUM
-- statement:
--   Let $x$ be an integer input of length $n$, stored below free base $f$. Supply a color program, entry point, resource function $R_c(n,U)$, and time bound $T_c$. Assume its color law: for every coloring and memory containing the exact input, nineteen parameter values, and six color fields, any call meeting $R_c$ returns the correct Boolean color-class witness and preserves memory below the color row.
--
--   Assume the driver's full resource record fits the machine limits at base $f$ and depth $d$. This record combines parameter initialization with the complete color-scan and backend requirements. The constructed program then returns a Boolean satisfying
--   $$r=1\iff\exists i,j,k<n\text{ pairwise distinct},\quad x_i+x_j+x_k=0.$$
--   It preserves all memory below $f$ and takes at most $T_{\rm init}(n)+T_{\rm scan}(T_c,n)+500$ steps.
--
--   This closes the outer driver by computing its own parameter header before scanning the complete color family.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/LightThreeSumFastDriver.lean#L59-L93

